> Here is the synopsis of a perspective I am writing:
> Synopsis:
> The social cost of carbon (SCC) has become a key metric in climate economics, representing the monetary value of the damages associated with an additional ton of CO2 emissions. The SCC has direct applications for government and institutions, to align climate policy to internalize the climate change externality and improve global social welfare. Even as changes in United States policy potentially reduce the legal role of the SCC there, the importance of the SCC for understanding risks and informing policy internationally is increasing with ever greater expected future climate risks. This review aims to elucidate the progress in understanding and applying the SCC, its critical role in policy-making, and avenues for future research. We discuss the current state of knowledge regarding how different climate impacts contribute to the total SCC and how estimates vary across models, emphasizing essential factors such as discounting methodologies and their relation to economic and ethical theories. Substantial uncertainties and debates surround the SCC, stemming from projections of long-term climate impacts, socio-economic trajectories, and ethical considerations in valuation methods. The future of SCC research involves tackling broader economic risks, the consequences of cascading climate impacts, and the role of inequality.
